    "result_text": "The test suite is green: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily create-owner limit for `POST /api/owners`: once 100 or more owners are already registered today (by `registrationDate`), a further create attempt is rejected with **429 Too Many Requests**.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (mirroring the existing `RejectCityAtCapacity` rule), I added:\n\n1. **`RejectDailyLimit.java`** \u2014 a pipeline step that counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` is today and throws `DailyLimitExceededException` when the count is \u2265 100. New owners default to today's registration date (set in `BuildOwner`), so this cap governs same-day signups.\n2. **`DailyLimitExceededException.java`** \u2014 the checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces as an escalation.\n3. **`DailyLimitExceededHandler.java`** \u2014 the escalation handler responding `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429).\n4. **`...DailyLimitExceededException.yml`** \u2014 escalation wiring mapping the exception to its handler.\n5. Wired `rejectDailyLimit` into `owners.POST.yml`, placed after the other rejection checks and before `build` (so it runs before any write/transaction, consistent with the other `Reject*` steps).\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit()` (create 100, expect the 101st to return 429) passes.",
